<Guide slug="make-a-classroom-label">
  <figure class="guide-figure">
    <img
      src="/studio/school-label.svg"
      alt="The Learning label starter before editing, with a blue frame, pencil, and simple decorations."
      width="800"
      height="600"
    />
    <figcaption>
      The actual Learning label starter. Add your own caption above the writing lines; the frame and
      decorations are separate editable pieces.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <h2>Decide what the label needs to say</h2>
  <p>
    Choose one purpose before changing the artwork. A notebook label might need only “Science”; a
    classroom tray might need “Paper scraps.” A short subject is easier to recognize than a sentence
    squeezed into a decorative frame. Keep student names out of any files or examples you send to us
    for support.
  </p>
  <p>
    This project uses the 800 × 600 Learning label starter. You will keep its frame, add a caption,
    remove any decoration that crowds the words, and save both an editable copy and an image for
    your layout. You do not need an account.
  </p>
  <h2>1. Open the starter and find its pieces</h2>
  <p>
    Open <a href="/create/">Create your own clipart</a> and choose <strong>Learning label</strong>.
    Selecting a starter replaces the current canvas; save an existing project first if you want to
    keep it separately. Open <strong>Layers</strong> to see the frame, pencil, decorations, and
    writing lines. Select a layer in that list when a small object is difficult to click on the
    canvas.
  </p>
  <p>
    The frame gives the words a quiet area. Try hiding one decorative layer before deleting it: this
    makes it easier to compare the simpler version with the original. Undo can restore a change, but
    a downloaded project file is a better way to keep a version for another day.
  </p>
  <h2>2. Give the words enough room</h2>
  <p>
    Open <strong>Text</strong>. Enter your wording in <strong>Caption</strong> and choose{' '}
    <strong>Add / update caption</strong>. Try “Science” first. Set the text color to a dark blue
    such as #1b293c and keep the space behind it pale. Color should support the words, not become
    the only way to identify the subject.
  </p>
  <p>
    If your wording overlaps the pencil, shorten it or move the decoration. Shrinking the letters
    repeatedly usually makes the label harder to use. Use the move controls or the keyboard arrow
    keys to adjust the selected layer; Shift plus an arrow moves it farther. Read the label at the
    size it will actually appear, not only while zoomed in.
  </p>
  <h2>3. Save a version you can change next term</h2>
  <p>
    Choose <strong>Save project</strong> to download the editable project. Use a filename you
    recognize, such as science-tray-label.json. A WebP export is a finished image and cannot restore
    the separate layers. Browser recovery is useful, but clearing site data or switching devices can
    remove access to that local copy.
  </p>
  <h2>4. Place the image without stretching it</h2>
  <p>
    Choose <strong>Export</strong>, select WebP, and keep the background transparent if you want the
    label to sit on colored paper or a document background. When inserting the exported image into
    your document, preserve its proportions. An 800 × 600 canvas has a 4:3 ratio, so a
    four-inch-wide image is three inches tall. The visible label inside it will be smaller because
    the canvas includes empty space.
  </p>
  <p>
    Use the <a href="/print-size/">print size calculator</a> with your actual exported pixel
    dimensions to check image density. If printing directly from the studio, select the paper and
    margins under <strong>Print setup</strong>, choose <strong>Fit entire artwork</strong>, and
    export PDF. This produces a page containing the artwork; it does not automatically arrange a
    sheet of repeated adhesive labels.
  </p>
  <h2>Before making a whole class set</h2>
  <p>
    Print one copy and inspect the smallest letters, frame, and pencil tip. Compare it with the
    intended tray or notebook. If a commercial label sheet has fixed cut lines, use that
    manufacturer's layout template and check its margins separately. The studio does not promise
    alignment with a particular label product.
  </p>
  <p>
    Classroom use is covered by our <a href="/license/">personal and educational license</a>.
    Selling labels made with our starter artwork is not permitted. For a design consisting entirely
    of your own original shapes, start on a blank canvas; UltraClipart does not claim ownership of
    that original work.
  </p>
</Guide>
